This is an example of solving in parallel
a linear time-dependent PDE with exponentially
fitted finite elements on an unstructured triangular mesh.
It is based on the PETSc Library and the metis mesh-partitioning tool.

***********************
CONTENTS:

1) Quick start
2) Description and options
3) Files

************************
1) QUICK START

a) To install and use this program
you will need the following software
installed:

- PETSc 2.3.0 
available from 
http://www-unix.mcs.anl.gov/petsc

- Metis 4.0
available from
http://www-users.cs.umn.edu/~karypis/metis

- GNU make
availaibe from
http://www.gnu.org

An implementation of the MPI message passing library
will be needed to build PETSc.
To run and postprocess the included
examples, you will allso need
to have matlab installed on your
system.

b) Define the following variables
in the environment or in the makefile:

PETSC_DIR, PETSC_ARCH (see petsc documentation)
MATLAB                full path to the matlab executable
METIS                 full path to the directory containing
                      METIS stand-alone executables (partnmesh etc.)

c) Run the following commands

make advdiffts  - to build the advdiffts binary
make examples   - to define the test problem data,
                  build the mesh and partition it
                  with METIS
make runexample - to run the included test example
                  on 2 processors.

************************
2) DESCRIPTION AND OPTIONS

The advdiffts program solves an Advection-Diffusion
equation of the form:

$$

\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} - 
\epsilon ( \triangle u - u \frac{\nabla V}{\epsilon} ) = 0

$$ 

with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions on the
boundary of the spatial doain $ \Omega $ and initial
conditions $ u(x,y,0) = u_0 $.

The spatial domain and its partitioned triangulation, 
the initial data, the potential field $V$ are
read by the program fro a set of ascii files:
p    - nodes of the mesh and node partitioning
t    - connectivity and element partitioning
bcs  - list of boundary nodes
data - potential, initial condition

The time step for ode solver, the maximum number of time steps,
the time stepping algorithm (euler, backward euler or crank-nicholson),
the coefficient epsilon are read from the following
command line options:

-deltat
-maxts
-ts_type
-epsilon

run the program with the -help option for more information
on these and other options.
All mpi and PETSc command-line options can also be used,
a typical command for calling advdiffts would look like:

mpirun -np 2 -nolocal advdiffts -prefix nproc2/ -ts_type beuler -epsilon 1e-6 -maxts 100 -deltat 0.1 -preload false

************************
3) FILES

The files included in this archive are

advdiffts.c     - c source code of the advdiff program
buildexamples.m,
buildmesh_metis.m,
plotseries.m    - matlab scripts to create the input data
                  and mesh for the examples and to plot the results
makefile        - makefile to build the program and the examples
README          - this file
LICENSE         - a copy of the GNU GPL license under wich this
                  program is released 